  "full_response": "Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that uses \"objects\" to design applications and computer programs. The key principles of OOP, often summarized by the acronym SOLID, are encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism. Here's a brief explanation of each, along with examples in Python:\n\n1. **Encapsulation**:\n   - Encapsulation is the bundling of data with the methods that operate on that data. It restricts direct access to some of an object's components, which is a means of preventing accidental interference and misuse of the data.\n\n   ```python\n   class Car:\n       def __init__(self, make, model):\n           self._make = make  # _ prefix is a convention to indicate a protected member\n           self._model = model\n       \n       def display_info(self):\n           print(f\"This car is a {self._make} {self._model}.\")\n   \n   my_car = Car(\"Toyota\", \"Corolla\")\n   my_car.display_info()\n   # Accessing _make directly is possible, but discouraged:\n   # print(my_car._make)  # Not recommended\n   ```\n\n2. **Abstraction**:\n   - Abstraction involves hiding the complex reality while exposing only the necessary parts. It allows you to focus on what the object does instead of how it does it.\n\n   ```python\n   from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\n   class Vehicle(ABC):\n       @abstractmethod\n       def max_speed(self):\n           pass\n\n   class Car(Vehicle):\n       def max_speed(self):\n           return 200  # A car's maximum speed\n\n   class Boat(Vehicle):\n       def max_speed(self):\n           return 80  # A boat's maximum speed\n\n   # Car and Boat implement the max_speed method defined in Vehicle.\n   ```\n\n3. **Inheritance**:\n   - Inheritance is a way to form new classes using classes that have already been defined. It provides a way to create a new class that is a modified version of an existing class.\n\n   ```python\n   class Vehicle:\n       def __init__(self, make, model):\n           self.make = make\n           self.model = model\n\n       def display_info(self):\n           print(f\"This vehicle is a {self.make} {self.model}.\")\n\n   class Car(Vehicle):\n       def __init__(self, make, model, horsepower):\n           super().__init__(make, model)\n           self.horsepower = horsepower\n\n       def display_car_info(self",
